        heading: 'The Control of Women’s Bodies',
        body:
          'Gilead is built on a single act of theft: it takes women’s bodies and reassigns them as state property. The regime sorts women by function and makes the sorting visible, dressing Wives in blue, Marthas in dull green, and Handmaids in the red of blood and fertility. Offred registers what this has done to her with terrible flatness, remembering that she once thought of her body as an instrument of pleasure or a means of transportation and knowing it has become instead a cloud congealed around the pear-shaped object at its center. "We are two-legged wombs," she says, and the flatness of the phrase is the measure of the damage.\n\nThe Ceremony makes the theft literal and, in Atwood’s handling, unbearably banal. Offred lies between Serena Joy’s knees while the Commander performs a duty the novel declines to call either sex or rape, since neither word quite fits an act she has technically chosen in order to stay alive. That impossible middle ground is the point. Gilead does not need to break every woman by force when it can arrange the alternatives, the Colonies or the Ceremony, so that consent becomes meaningless while still existing on paper.\n\nWhat keeps the theme from floating into abstraction is Atwood’s attention to how quickly it all happened. Offred remembers the day her card was refused at the corner store and the day her job ended by memo, and she remembers doing nothing, because the change arrived as paperwork rather than as soldiers. Control over reproduction is presented here not as an exotic horror but as an administrative one, achieved with a keystroke and defended afterward with scripture.',
