          'Atwood is precise about her genre and impatient with the label science fiction. *The Handmaid’s Tale*, she argues, is speculative fiction, and for her that carries one binding rule: nothing goes into Gilead that human beings have not already done somewhere, to someone. The epigraph makes the method plain by opening in Genesis, where the barren Rachel gives her maid Bilhah to Jacob so that a child may be born upon her knees. Gilead does not invent the Ceremony. It looks an old one up and enforces it with a bureaucracy.\n\nThe rest of the regime is assembled the same way, from parts already in the historical record. Its public executions and the bodies displayed on the Wall are older than any modern dictatorship. Its group punishments and staged confessions belong squarely to the twentieth century. Its pronatalism echoes states that criminalized contraception to raise a birth rate, and its traffic in children echoes regimes that took the young from disfavoured parents and handed them to the loyal. The American setting is not accidental either: Atwood dedicated the book in part to a Puritan ancestor and placed her coup in New England, the ground where an English theocracy was actually built.\n\nThis is precisely what makes the novel so hard to shake off. A fantasy can be filed away as invention, but a recombination cannot. Each time a reader decides the regime has finally gone too far to be credible, the book quietly answers that some version of it already has a citation, and the horror shifts from what people might one day do to what they have proved willing to do already.',
