          'No novel of the past fifty years has been carried into the street more often than this one. Since the United States Supreme Court removed federal constitutional protection for abortion in 2022, demonstrators at statehouses and courthouses have appeared in the red cloak and white bonnet, standing in silent rows, and the costume works precisely because it says nothing out loud. It is a wearable argument that a legal system can define a woman by her fertility, and by 2026 campaigners in Ireland, Poland, Argentina, and elsewhere have adopted it long past the point where it needed any explanation.\n\nWhat the novel contributes to the argument is less a prediction than a set of questions. Gilead does not begin by outlawing anything dramatic; it begins by deciding that a fertile body is a public resource, then reasons its way to everything else. Readers debating today who may prescribe a medication, who may cross a state line for care, and who may see a patient’s own medical records are arguing about the same underlying question the novel poses: whether a person’s reproductive life belongs to her or to the state that counts her.\n\nAtwood herself has resisted letting the book harden into a slogan, describing it as a warning rather than a prophecy. That distinction is worth keeping. The novel is most useful now not as a description of any actual government but as a device for testing arguments, asking of each new proposal what it assumes about whose body is at stake and who gets to decide.',
