          'For a great many readers outside Afghanistan, this novel was the first time the country appeared as a place with kite tournaments, poetry evenings, and a middle class, rather than as a dateline. Hosseini moves through four decades of upheaval by way of one family, from the last calm years of the monarchy to the Soviet invasion, the warlords, and the arrival of the Taliban, and the technique matters: history registers as the sound of a rocket landing on a neighbour’s house, or a father bribing a soldier at a checkpoint. That human scale explains why the book returned to bestseller lists after 2021, when the Taliban took Kabul again and readers reached for something that felt like context.\n\nThe American chapters may be even more useful in 2026. Baba, once a man who built an orphanage, arrives in California and works nights at a gas station, and his fury at the welfare office, where he slams the offered food stamps on the counter and insists that he works, captures a loss of status that resettlement statistics never convey. Millions of Afghans remain displaced, many of them in Iran and Pakistan under pressure to return to a country where girls are barred from secondary school and university, and the novel gives a reader some idea of what a passport line and a flea-market stall feel like from the inside.\n\nHosseini himself has spent the years since publication working on refugee advocacy, and the book has become a standard text in classrooms trying to teach displacement without reducing it to policy. Its real contribution is refusal of caricature. The same country contains Assef and Hassan, the stadium and the kite string, and a reader who finishes the novel is far less able to speak about Afghans as a single undifferentiated mass.',
