      '*Night* is Elie Wiesel’s account of his own deportation, as a teenage Jewish boy from Sighet in Transylvania, to Auschwitz and Buchenwald. It opens in 1941 with Moishe the Beadle, a poor synagogue caretaker who is expelled as a foreign Jew, escapes a massacre, and comes back to Sighet to describe what he saw. Nobody believes him. The town decides he has lost his mind, and the ordinary business of life continues through the yellow stars, the two ghettos, and the requisitioned houses, until the cattle cars arrive in the spring of 1944. On the train, a woman named Madame Schächter screams that she can see flames, and the other passengers beat her quiet; hours later they reach Birkenau and smell the chimneys.\n\nThe selection on the ramp separates Eliezer and his father from his mother and his little sister Tzipora, whom he never sees again. A stranger tells him to lie about his age, and that lie keeps him alive. What follows is a record of a world designed to remove everything a person is: his name becomes the number A-7713 tattooed on his arm, his prayers dry up, and the daily arithmetic of survival narrows to bread, soup, and the effort of standing upright during roll call. He watches a young boy hanged so light that he strangles slowly, and that evening the soup tastes of corpses. He watches a son beat his father for a crust on a transport, and understands the impulse well enough to be terrified of it.\n\nWhen the Red Army approaches, the SS force the prisoners to run through the snow to Gleiwitz and then on to Buchenwald. Eliezer’s father, sick with dysentery and beaten for asking for water, dies in late January 1945, weeks before the Americans arrive. Eliezer does not answer when his father calls his name in the night, and he admits that somewhere beneath the grief he felt something like relief, a confession that costs him more than any description of the camps. Liberation comes on 11 April 1945. In the final paragraph he looks into a mirror for the first time since the ghetto and sees a corpse looking back, an image that has never left him.',
