          'The camps in Wiesel’s account work by subtraction. Clothes, hair, and possessions go first, then the name, replaced by the number A-7713 which he is told is now his only identity. He describes prisoners reduced to bodies competing for bread, men who would kill for a mouthful of soup, and a self that shrinks until it contains almost nothing but hunger and the instinct to stay upright at roll call. The prose is deliberately plain, because ornament would let the reader off.\n\nThe cruellest part of the design is what it does to love. Wiesel notices, again and again, that the system rewards abandonment: Rabbi Eliahu’s son runs ahead on the march so the old man cannot slow him down, a son on the transport beats his father to death for a scrap of bread, and Eliezer catches himself calculating that his father’s rations would keep him alive longer. When his father dies and he feels something like release, he does not excuse it or explain it away. He writes it down, which is the bravest sentence in the book.\n\nThe title names the result. Night in this memoir is not merely the setting of the arrival or the death march but the condition that descends on the human spirit when a system has been built to extinguish it. The last image, the corpse staring out of the mirror, tells the reader that the boy who left Sighet did not survive, whatever the liberation records say. Wiesel’s survival is real, and so is his knowledge that something in him did not come back.',
