        body:
          'The generation that can say "I was there" is very nearly gone. By 2026 the youngest survivors are in their eighties and nineties, and within a few years Holocaust education will rely entirely on recordings, archives, and books like this one. That transition is happening at exactly the wrong moment, as surveys in several countries report that large numbers of young adults cannot identify Auschwitz and antisemitic incidents climb in Europe and North America.\n\n*Night* answers denial in a way that statistics cannot, because it is small, specific, and impossible to argue with in the abstract. It gives a reader Moishe the Beadle, Madame Schächter, Juliek playing Beethoven in the dark at Gleiwitz, and a father asking for water. Denial and distortion depend on keeping the six million abstract; the memoir works by refusing abstraction, one named person at a time.\n\nThe threat now moves faster than it did in Wiesel’s lifetime. Distorted images and invented history circulate online at a speed no archive can match, and synthetic video makes fabricated testimony cheap to produce. In that environment an authenticated firsthand account, written by a man who spent his life defending the record, is not a relic of an older curriculum. It is a primary source, and reading it carefully is a form of resistance.',
