        heading: 'The Danger of Indifference',
        body:
          'The single most quoted sentence Wiesel ever spoke is his insistence that the opposite of love is not hatred but indifference, and *Night* is where that conviction begins. Sighet does not fall because its neighbours are monsters. It falls because a warning arrives and everyone decides it cannot be true, because the Germans in the first weeks seem polite, and because there is always a reason to wait a little longer before acting. The book is a study of the interval between a warning and a catastrophe, and of what people do with it.\n\nThat interval is where readers in 2026 find themselves repeatedly. Famines, mass displacement, and atrocities are reported now with more evidence and more immediacy than at any point in history, and the abundance itself becomes an anaesthetic; one more feed, one more headline, one more scroll past. Wiesel’s point was never that individuals can stop a genocide by caring. It was that indifference is a decision, taken by many people at once, and that it does measurable work on the side of the perpetrator.\n\nHe was equally clear that neutrality is not a moral position, arguing that silence always helps the tormentor rather than the tormented. That is an uncomfortable standard to apply to anyone’s ordinary week, which is precisely why the memoir keeps being assigned. It does not ask the reader to imagine being a victim. It asks the reader to consider being a citizen of Sighet in 1943, listening to a man nobody wanted to believe.',
