        heading: 'Dreams Deferred',
        body:
          'The title comes from Langston Hughes, whose poem "Harlem" asks what happens to a dream deferred and offers a series of possibilities: it dries up like a raisin in the sun, it festers, it sags, or it explodes. Hansberry assigns each option to a different member of one household. Mama’s dream has been drying for decades, ever since she and Big Walter picked out a little place they meant to buy within the year and then spent a lifetime in this apartment instead. Walter’s has festered into resentment he takes out on everyone within reach. Ruth’s has sagged into exhaustion.\n\nThe genius of the play is that these dreams are not compatible. The same ten thousand dollars cannot become a house, a liquor store, and a medical degree, so the family fights over a sum that was never large enough for all of them. Hansberry is careful to show why it is so small: it exists only because a man worked himself to death, and when Mama says that freedom used to be life and now it is money, Walter answers that it was always money and they simply did not know it. Poverty has forced the family into a zero-sum argument, and the argument then looks like a personal failing.\n\nHughes’s last option is the explosion, and Walter provides it, though not in the way an audience expects. The blow-up is not a riot but a betrayal, sixty-five hundred dollars handed to a man who was never coming back, a sister’s tuition included. What Hansberry finally suggests is that deferred dreams do not simply wither on their own; they are deferred by particular arrangements, by wages and mortgages and neighbourhood associations, and the play insists that the audience see the machinery rather than blame the dreamer.',
