        heading: 'Housing Discrimination and the Racial Wealth Gap',
        body:
          'The Clybourne Park plot was not invented. Hansberry’s own family bought a house in a white Chicago neighbourhood when she was a child, faced a hostile crowd and a brick through the window, and fought the restrictive covenant that barred them all the way to the United States Supreme Court, where the case was decided in 1940. Karl Lindner, with his coffee-table manners and his talk of the right of a community to protect its character, is drawn from life, and Hansberry makes him polite on purpose. The play understands that most housing discrimination has been conducted by pleasant people with paperwork.\n\nThat paperwork is the reason the play still explains 2026. Covenants, redlining maps, and the exclusion of Black families from the postwar mortgage boom did their work at exactly the moment when American homeownership was creating middle-class wealth, and the effects compound across generations. Contemporary studies of appraisal bias, mortgage denial rates, and the racial gap in household wealth are describing the long tail of the world Lindner represents, and the gap in median wealth between Black and white households remains enormous.\n\nSeen through that lens, Mama’s down payment is not sentimental. It is a bid for the single asset most American families use to pass something to their children, made by a woman who understands the arithmetic perfectly. The Youngers move into Clybourne Park knowing that the neighbourhood does not want them, which is why the last scene feels like courage rather than a happy ending.',
