        body:
          'The first thing that happens to Marji in this book is that a piece of cloth is placed on her head by law, and the last four decades of Iranian politics have kept returning to that same object. When protests spread across Iran after the death in custody of Mahsa Amini in 2022, the slogan that carried them, "Woman, Life, Freedom," described a struggle Satrapi had already drawn: women arguing with patrols about a strand of hair, and a state that treats female appearance as a matter of national security.\n\nWhat the memoir supplies for a reader following the news in 2026 is interiority. Satrapi shows the arithmetic Iranian women perform before leaving the house, the double life that a private party requires, and the way a regime’s rules become absurd and lethal at the same time. She also shows what an authoritarian state does to a generation over time, following her friends through exile, prison, marriage, and silence rather than freezing them at the moment of protest.\n\nThe book is careful not to flatter the West either. Marji returns from Europe to find that the country she left has real love, humour, and stubbornness in it, and Satrapi has spent her public life resisting the idea that Iranians need rescuing. That is a useful corrective to coverage that reduces a large, ancient, argumentative country to whichever crisis has reached the front page this month.',
