        body:
          'The novel’s architecture is its argument. Sixteen first-person narratives are arranged in four sections, each opening with a short parable, and the voices alternate between generations and continents so that a mother’s Chinese past sits directly beside her daughter’s American present. There is no omniscient narrator to sort it out. The reader assembles the connections that the characters themselves cannot see, which puts the reader in the position of the family historian nobody in the family has yet become.\n\nThe pattern-making rewards attention. Ying-ying’s childhood loss at the Moon Festival, when she is separated from her family and stops believing anyone will find her, prefigures the passivity that later paralyses Lena in her marriage. Lindo’s escape from a marriage she never chose explains the strategic ferocity that makes Waverly a chess prodigy and a difficult daughter. An-mei’s silent mother stands behind Rose’s silence in a divorce lawyer’s office. Nothing is spelled out; the stories simply rhyme.\n\nThe frame completes the design. June sits down at the mahjong table in her mother’s place at the beginning of the book and, at the end, stands on Chinese soil in her mother’s place as well, telling her half-sisters a story she has only just learned herself. Identity in this novel is not something a person invents; it is something handed forward, imperfectly, and the structure of the book is the shape of that handing forward.',
