        body:
          'The daughters in this novel occupy a position that now has a name and an enormous online literature. They are fluent in a culture their parents entered as adults, embarrassed by the accents at home, and quietly aware that they are the translators for their own families. In 2026 the same experience is discussed in the language of second-generation and third-culture identity, and much of what gets described in essays and short videos, the shame that later turns into grief, was mapped by Tan more than thirty-five years ago.\n\nThe novel is also a landmark in publishing history. It spent months on bestseller lists, sold in the millions, and made a mainstream American audience willing to read Chinese American family stories, opening a door that later writers walked through. That matters in 2026, when arguments about who gets published and reviewed remain live, and when a single breakout book is still expected to represent an entire community. Tan resisted that expectation, insisting she was writing about eight specific women rather than about all Chinese Americans.\n\nWhat keeps the book from being merely historical is its ending. June does not resolve her identity by choosing a side; she goes to China, meets sisters she cannot speak to without an interpreter, and recognizes her mother in their faces. Belonging in this novel is not a country or a passport but a line of transmission, which is a description many readers of immigrant families in 2026 find more accurate than either of the labels on offer.',
